{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792795283,"nonce":"bxWBWndCv2U/MtPixDJlHecWHFA=","title":"TypeScript: optional, default and rest parameter calls","message":"Functions, methods and constructors take optional and default parameters of number or boolean type and a rest parameter. An omittable parameter is a native optional slot that a caller fills with undefined when it leaves the argument out; the callee evaluates a default on entry, after the parameters before it, so defaults may use them. A rest parameter receives an array the caller builds from its trailing arguments. A parameter written after an optional one must be omittable, as TypeScript requires (1016, a checker error). Gaps reported as Unsupported: other parameter types, string defaults without an annotation, function values and exports of such functions, optional parameter properties, and logical assignment operators (which reached a false number check). Official corpus: no verdict changes (PASS 947, FAIL 97, UNSUPPORTED 2360, SKIP 3421); the rows these features unblock stop at other gaps. Local fixtures 1311/1311.","files":null}]}